<p class="copy">
	St-1/E.003 forms the first half of the <?php echo sf_lore_link('fuel-gates', 'fuel gate'); ?>/water gate duopoly of technologies that underpin space travel and colonization in the settled systems. Named after its paraphysical effect, whereby the element experiences much-accelerated chemical synthesis in combination reactions, Fast Hydrogen is a common sight in heavy manufacturing, chemical processing, and military applications. Its best-known use, though, is as rocket fuel, where its heightened reactivity allows for far greater thrust and higher specific impulse.
</p>
<p class="copy mb-0">
	Handling Fast Hydrogen is an exercise in paranoia. Its mundane cousin is already infamous for slipping through the smallest flaw in a seal; Fast Hydrogen slips through just as easily, and burns far more eagerly. The element can react with oxygen at temperatures as low as 32°C, resulting in uncontrolled burn-offs at even the slightest leak, and that’s to say nothing of what the element does to a living body unlucky enough to come into direct contact with it. Severe burns on the skin, flash fires within the respiratory tract, and even micro-explosions within the bloodstream have been recorded in instances where the element has mixed in trace amounts with the warm, oxygen-rich air of a ship. It’s perhaps fortunate, then, that Fast Hydrogen has a paraphysical decay half-life of roughly thirty hours, transmuting harmlessly into mundane hydrogen.
</p>
